

1. Core idea

Product sentence:

A blueprint simulation engine where encryption systems are designed, simulated, scored, and visualized as living blueprints, under the CMB meta‑framework.

So instead of “here’s an AES implementation”, you have:

a blueprint that describes AES‑like structure,

a simulator that explores its behavior and cost,

a meta‑layer that compares it to other designs.

2. Blueprint layer (your encryption DSL)

This is the language of the universe.

Atoms: primitive ops, algebraic structures, randomness.

Constructions: SPN, Feistel, sponge, stream generators, key schedules, modes.

Schemes: “CMB_AES_LIKE”, “CMB_STREAM_LIKE”, “CMB_POST_QUANTUM_LIKE”.

Policies: security level, performance target, side‑channel profile.

Everything is a declarative blueprint, not code.

cipher CMB_AES_LIKE {
  primitive: SPN;
  block_size: 128;
  key_size: 256;

  round {
    sub_bytes(SBOX_CMB);
    shift_rows(pattern = spiral);
    mix_columns(matrix = M_CMB);
    add_round_key(schedule = K_CMB);
  }

  mode: GCM;
  profile: CMB_SECURE_BALANCED;
}

3. Simulation layer (the “engine” part)

This is where “simulation software” becomes literal.

Static analyzers:

round‑reduction margin vs generic attacks,

diffusion metrics, avalanche, linearity/nonlinearity scores,

key schedule strength, state size, etc.

Cost models:

estimated cycles on target architectures,

memory footprint, code size,

parallelizability.

Side‑channel models (abstract):

leakage surfaces (table lookups vs ARX),

masking/hardening cost estimates.

The engine takes a blueprint and produces a state vector:

[ S = { \text{security}, \text{latency}, \text{throughput}, \text{footprint}, \text{side_channel_risk}, \dots } ]

4. Meta‑equation layer (CMB signature)

Here lives “C MATTHEW BLACKWELL INDEPENDENT DESIGNER” as math.

Scoring functional:

[ \text{Score}(\text{cipher}) = w_1 \cdot \text{security} - w_2 \cdot \text{latency} - w_3 \cdot \text{footprint} - w_4 \cdot \text{risk} ]

Profiles:

CMB_MAX_SECURITY

CMB_BALANCED

CMB_ULTRALIGHT

Search/evolution:

given a profile, the engine can:

tweak parameters,

swap atoms (e.g., S‑box families),

change round counts,

and search for higher‑scoring designs.

This is your meta‑equation governing the whole design space.

5. Visualization layer (blueprint as living diagram)

Because you’re a designer, not just a theorist:

Structural view: rounds, flows, key schedule as a circuit/graph.

Field view: diffusion/avalanche visualized as spreading patterns.

Trade‑off plots: security vs performance vs footprint.

So each cipher blueprint is both:

a diagram you can read,

and a simulated object you can interrogate.

